592186049059 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050697 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050697 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050697 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186100884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050697 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186100885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050697 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186100960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050697 #edb/ref 1031 "The Power Rule for Exponents" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050697 #edb/ref 1033 1224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050697 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "5faccef4-6874-58a8-9306-2498a05448ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115029 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4101" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115029 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve for $x$ where $3^{3x-3}=9^x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115029 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "baae925b-d23d-56db-b15f-b438f68df608" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115029 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base.\n\nTo do this, we write $9$ as ${\\color{red}3}^2,$ and then rewrite the right-hand side using the power rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{3x-3} & ={9}^{x} \\\\ {3}^{3x-3} & ={({3}^{2})}^{x} \\\\ {3}^{3x-3} & ={3}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $({\\color{red}3}),$ we equate the exponents and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-3 & =2x \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115048 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4161" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115048 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve for $x$ where $9^{3x}=27^{x+2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115048 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e73675af-973f-583e-9909-0eb9630fdcc8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115048 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $9={\\color{red}3}^2$ and $27 = {\\color{red}3}^3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left({\\color{red}3}^2\\right)^{3x}=\\left({\\color{red}3}^3\\right)^{x+2}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2(3x)}={3}^{3(x+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $({\\color{red}3}),$ we equate the exponents and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(3x) & =3(x+2) \\\\ 6x & =3x+6 \\\\ 3x & =6 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115200 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115200 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115200 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115200 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115200 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115200 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234212 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115200 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115200 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115200 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115200 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115200 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115200 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115200 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving Exponential Equations Containing Different Bases by Rewriting One Exponent" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115200 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186115029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115200 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "9c6b30e7-fba6-597c-8306-27d707bfc244" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115219 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115219 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186115945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115219 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115219 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115219 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115219 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115219 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115219 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115219 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115219 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115219 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115219 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115219 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186547999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115219 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving Exponential Equations Containing Different Bases by Rewriting Two Exponents" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115219 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186115048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115219 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "a8eebff2-97ca-599b-a819-04ff34f0c1c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115417 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115417 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115417 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115417 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115417 #edb/ref 1045 "Solving Exponential Equations With Different Bases" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115417 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186116060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115417 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115417 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115417 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186115417 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "e6144229-d720-5bc8-9e1a-98e1705bfbbe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116060 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116060 #edb/ref 1061 14505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116060 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "19d6b9e6-6845-5508-9a53-13a6d18de9d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116060 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116061 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116061 #edb/ref 1061 14516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116061 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "43796675-9206-5f0a-a634-378038c51c7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116061 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116087 #edb/ref 1061 15049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116087 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bd0d0141-6460-5be8-8511-503ece684172" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116087 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116478 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we are given the equation $3^{3x} = 81^{x-1},$ which has two exponential terms. How do we solve it?\n\nTo solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using the fact that $81 = {\\color{red}3}^4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}3}^{3x}=\\left({\\color{red}3}^4\\right)^{x-1}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{3x} & ={3}^{4(x-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $({\\color{red}3}),$ we equate the exponents and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =4(x-1) \\\\ 3x & =4x-4 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhenever we can rewrite an equation so that both sides have a common base, we can solve the equation by equating the exponents." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116478 #edb/ref 1065 2798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116478 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "91b0d933-a432-59bd-b187-7dd052926b12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116478 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333640 #edb/ref 1080 "-15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186333640 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [