7999 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $9={3}^{2}$ and $27={3}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{({3}^{2})}^{y}={({3}^{3})}^{y-3}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule of exponents. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2y}={3}^{3(y-3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(3)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y & =3(y-3) \\\\ 2y & =3y-9 \\\\ y & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548013 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548013 #edb/ref 1069 "q-26373" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548013 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548013 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${27}^{x+2}={3}^{4x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548013 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6adee969-6d90-563b-a4ef-2a55fc2a8432" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548013 #edb/ref 1074 "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base.\n\nTo do this, we write $27$ as ${3}^{3},$ and then rewrite the left-hand side using the power rule of exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{27}^{x+2} & ={3}^{4x} \\\\ {({3}^{3})}^{x+2} & ={3}^{4x} \\\\ {3}^{3(x+2)} & ={3}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(3)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(x+2) & =4x \\\\ 3x+6 & =4x \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
